    "step5_reality_check": {
      "0": "Screenshots show two relevant content sources: (a) the repository main page where the README is rendered inline (including the ‘Supports macOS, Linux, and Windows.’ line and the Installation commands), and (b) a specific release tag page that shows the release header (tag, “Latest” badge, and “Released on …” date) plus per-release installer snippets and asset listings. The repo page screenshots also show root-level files including README.md, LICENSE-APACHE, and LICENSE-MIT, but do not show the actual license file contents. Therefore, README-based claims can be tied to the rendered README section on the repo page, and release tag/date can be tied to the release page; license claims based strictly on license text would require opening the license files (not shown in the provided evidence).",
      "1": "The README as rendered on the repository page explicitly states: “Supports macOS, Linux, and Windows.” No other platforms are shown in the README excerpt captured in the screenshots, so scoring should treat those three as the documented supported OS/platforms in the provided evidence.",
      "2": "Two different Linux/macOS-style installer commands appear depending on the page: the repository README shows a generic command using `https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh`, while the specific release tag page shows a version-pinned installer script URL and extra curl flags (`--proto --tlsv1.2`, and `uv-installer.sh` under a release download path). Both are ‘documented’ commands, but they are evidenced on different GitHub pages; an answer should match the command from the page it cites.",
      "3": "Similarly, Windows installation is documented in two places with different commands: the repository README shows a generic PowerShell command using `https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1` (with ExecutionPolicy ByPass/Bypass), while the release tag page shows a version-specific PowerShell installer script URL (`uv-installer.ps1` under the release download path). As with Linux/macOS, an answer must align with the specific page used as evidence.",
      "4": "The README excerpt visible in the screenshots shows “Or, from PyPI:” followed by two alternatives: `pip install uv` and `pipx install uv`. The captured README content appears truncated in at least one screenshot (indicating hidden blocks), so it’s possible other installation methods exist elsewhere in the README, but only pip and pipx are explicitly evidenced in the shown README section.",
      "5": "The release tag page screenshot for version 0.12.5 explicitly shows a “Latest” badge and the text “Released on 2026-08-14,” plus a timestamp line (“released this 14 Aug 19:57”). No prerelease label is visible on that page in the provided evidence, so it appears to be a stable release as presented. The general releases list view is not evidenced in the provided screenshots, so ‘latest’ is supported here only by the presence of the “Latest” badge on the tag page.",
      "6": "The repository file list in screenshots shows two license files, LICENSE-APACHE and LICENSE-MIT, and the sidebar summary indicates “Apache-2.0, MIT licenses found.” However, none of the screenshots show the opened contents/text of either license file. So the evidence supports the presence of dual license files and GitHub’s detected license labels, but not a verbatim confirmation from the license text itself.",
      "7": "Across the screenshots, installation commands are only displayed as documentation snippets (README/release page) with no indication of execution. A visible “Sign in” option and ‘You must be signed in…’ notices indicate unauthenticated browsing. The release page shown does not display a prerelease marker. The main ‘stop after every answer has been tied to a specific GitHub page or repository file’ condition hinges on whether each reported item is backed by a shown GitHub page: README items are evidenced on the repo page, release tag/date on the release tag page, but license-from-file-contents would require opening license files (not shown)."
    },
